    "result_text": "All rejection responses (400, 409, 429) are produced exclusively through the escalation handlers, and every one now emits an RFC7807 `ProblemDetail`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe task required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body carrying `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\n**What was already compliant:** The 409 handlers (`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`, `DuplicateOwnerExceptionHandler`), the 429 handler (`TooManyOwnersTodayExceptionHandler`), and the bean-validation 400 handler (`ValidationExceptionHandler`) already built their bodies via the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper, which sets `type` (`about:blank`), `title` (exception simple name), `status` (from the HTTP status), and `detail`.\n\n**What I changed** \u2014 six 400 handlers that still returned ad-hoc `{\"error\": ...}` / `{\"errors\": [...]}` JSON:\n- `DisposableEmailDomainExceptionHandler`\n- `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler`\n- `InvalidEmailExceptionHandler`\n- `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler`\n- `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler`\n- `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler`\n\nEach now returns `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` built through `ProblemDetails.build(ex, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, ex.getMessage())`, so the response is `application/problem+json` with the four required members. For `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler` I preserved the missing-field list as an extra `errors` property on the problem body (RFC7807 permits additional members).\n\nHTTP status codes are unchanged, and no other code path produces a 400/409/429.\n\n**Verification:** The new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (missing `city` \u2192 `MissingOwnerFieldsException`) now asserts status 400, `contentTypeCompatibleWith(\"application/problem+json\")`, and `$.status == 400` \u2014 it passes. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0).",
